SF Unlock Blueprints is a ShroudForge mod that changes the unlock requirements stored on Enshrouded recipes. Its package ID is <code>sf-unlock-blueprints</code>, and the current package version is <code>1.0.2</code>.

The package is disabled by default. It targets the client and Dedicated Server and requests the <code>patch</code> capability. It does not request the Lua <code>runtime</code> capability and has no player-facing settings.

== What the mod changes ==

During ShroudForge's pregame asset-preparation pass, the mod reads the game's named knowledge query <code>Unlock_Flame_Altar_PK</code> and resolves the knowledge ID used by its <code>NPC_Flame_Hint01</code> action. It does not assume that this ID is a universal constant.

The mod then scans the loaded <code>keen::RecipeRegistryResource</code> assets. For each recipe with a knowledge requirement, it sets that requirement to the resolved Flame Altar hint and normalizes the requirement to a simple Boolean equality check. Recipes without a knowledge requirement are left alone. The package does not change recipe ingredients, outputs, production times, or recipes that have no unlock requirement.

The change takes effect after the game starts with the mod enabled. If ShroudForge finds no recipe registries, it reports a warning. If registries are present but no recipe needs a change, it reports that result. Otherwise, it logs the number of changed recipes and registries.

== Enable the mod ==

# Install the <code>sf-unlock-blueprints</code> package in the ShroudForge mods directory for the process that loads the world.
# Open the ShroudForge Mods window and enable SF Unlock Blueprints.
# Restart Enshrouded so the pregame asset-preparation pass can update recipe requirements.
# Check the ShroudForge log if the expected recipes remain locked. Look for the package's registry and changed-recipe messages.

The package uses the <code>patch</code> capability to alter game asset data before normal gameplay. If that capability is unavailable, the mod stops with an error instead of continuing without applying its change.

== Reverting the change ==

The package manifest warns that reverting this change requires resetting all affected blueprint unlock progress. Do not disable the mod in an existing world unless you are prepared to reset that progress. Back up the world and review the current package release before changing the mod's state.

